---
/**
 * OUR COPY REWRITTEN TO SS_WRITING_FOR_KASPAR.md, 26 Nov 2026 (a founder: the
 * remaining area pages rewritten to the writing Kaspar comments on; ops rulings
 * D3, no "carpentry" in our copy, and D7). Only our words changed. No word of
 * Kaspar's changed: his 6 Sep lede sentence, his 10 Nov lede sentences, his four
 * 8 Nov sections and his 10 Nov rewrites read as he wrote them, apart from the
 * two changes already named to him ("seamless" to "neat", 12 Nov; "Absolutely."
 * cut, Denis, 21 Nov). Our lines that changed:
 *   - Lede, first sentence: our "and other bespoke carpentry" (15 Nov) is out
 *     (D3), so it reads as his 6 Sep sentence again.
 *   - Lede, second paragraph: our "Every piece of bespoke joinery" (15 Nov) is
 *     back to his "Every project". The review card that asked him about both
 *     changes is obsolete.
 *   - Section labels: "Fit" is now "Period homes and mansion flats", and
 *     "Listing and consent" is now "Listed homes", following his headings as on
 *     Camden and Islington.
 *   - Our headings (K17): "What does fitted furniture cost?" is now "Guide prices
 *     for fitted furniture in Hampstead"; "What guarantee comes with the work?"
 *     is now "What guarantee comes with our fitted furniture?".
 *   - The three renders: no description printed under them (K20; a founder,
 *     24 Nov); each alt text cut to one sentence about the joinery; the caption
 *     prop no longer reads "AI-generated example" (it never printed; the short
 *     name shows as before).
 *   - The three FAQ answers, rewritten in his register from his facts only: the
 *     material range and "how the furniture will be used" (his materials page,
 *     6 Sep), the measured survey before design (his process stages), Blum and
 *     Häfele (Q28), soft-close hinges as standard (his 31 Aug starting
 *     specification), doors and drawers that run properly and adjust (his
 *     materials page), kitchens from £10,000 (his 8 Nov card). The questions
 *     themselves are unchanged.
 * Left as they were: the price cards and their lines, the two lines above them,
 * the guarantee paragraph (its wording is open for a founder across the area
 * pages), the line to the two guides, the labels "Design", "In your building",
 * "Access", "Guide prices", "Guarantee" and "Questions".
 *
 * Hampstead, the merged area page, 9 November 2026. Built hidden; released
 * 12 November 2026 once Kaspar had rewritten the new copy (see THE GATE).
 *
 * WHY MERGED. Two versions went to Kaspar: this page on 6 Nov and a design-led
 * copy at /carpenter-hampstead-design/ on 7 Nov. Five independent reviews and
 * three skeptics found this one the better base for search (ops repo PR #32,
 * deliverables/area-pages/carpenter-hampstead-v1-v2-merge-plan.md), and a
 * founder asked on 9 Nov for the merged page: Step 1 (this page plus Kaspar's
 * 8 Nov answers) and Step 2 (a short Hampstead design section, which his own
 * heading and note for the design-led copy asked for).
 *
 * WHOSE WORDS.
 *   - Kaspar's, word for word: the four sections he rewrote on 8 Nov (period
 *     houses, how the work runs, conservation areas and listed buildings,
 *     parking and access), headings included, and the design section's heading
 *     and first paragraph. Tone protocol v1.4 section 1: the client wins. Where
 *     his words break one of the protocol's hard bans (a 9 Nov check found six,
 *     in all four sections), they go back to him rather than being reworded:
 *     the preview's question cards ask him to keep or drop them (F2F repo,
 *     clients/structure-and-style/hampstead-preview/questions.json). What he
 *     keeps is logged as a section 16 exception; what he drops comes out here.
 *     Nothing of his is reworded here.
 *   - Kaspar's again, 10 Nov 2026: on the preview he rewrote every line of new
 *     copy we had held for his yes (the design section's body, the parking
 *     line, the drawings and process lines) and the lede's second sentence.
 *     His words replace ours as he wrote them; the only additions are links on
 *     words he wrote ("parking zones", "trade permits", "Our process", the
 *     service names). His tone-guide breaks are logged as section 16
 *     exceptions in the ops repo, not reworded.
 *   - The approved first-version copy, unchanged: the lede's first sentence,
 *     the cost list, the guarantee and the four questions.
 *   - Ours, which he read and left as it was: the line to the two guides that
 *     closes the design section.
 *   - Cut, 13 Nov 2026 (a founder): the design section's opening sentence on
 *     Hampstead's architectural styles, a near repeat of his Fit section
 *     sentence. The removed words are kept in a comment where they stood.
 *
 * THE GATE. areaHampsteadMerge held the new copy until he approved it. He
 * answered on 10 Nov by rewriting it, so the key is answered and the page is
 * indexable: in the sitemap, in the footer's Areas group and linked from the
 * where-we-work lines. areaHampstead1 (Hampstead jobs) was dropped on 9 Nov by
 * a founder's ruling, and the design-led copy at /carpenter-hampstead-design/
 * was deleted with this release (netlify.toml forwards its address here).
 *
 * Street and building names (Church Row, Keats Grove, Downshire Hill,
 * Willoughby Road, Northcote Mansions) are Kaspar's words and Camden's
 * conservation area statement's, not places we claim to have worked. The three
 * renders are the journal guides' files with their approved alt text, each
 * captioned with what it shows. Since 19 Nov 2026 (a founder) neither the
 * pictures nor the page say they are AI-generated examples.
 *
 * ON THE PRIMROSE HILL MODEL, 15 Nov 2026 (a founder's go; Denis: "use the
 * same design and structure for the next articles"; ops README, "The structure
 * rule, applied"). The address moved to /areas/hampstead/, with 301s from
 * /carpenter-hampstead/ and /carpenter-hampstead-design/, and the page is held
 * by areaHampsteadCheck until Kaspar has read it: none of it reaches the live
 * site before he approves every area page (Denis, 15 Nov). Title, heading,
 * kicker and description lead with bespoke wardrobes (areas.ts). His words are
 * kept as they stand, with two changes for the model, both in the lede: "and
 * other bespoke carpentry" added to the approved first sentence, and his
 * "Every project" now "Every piece of bespoke joinery". Added: a line drawing
 * beside the Fit section (a mansion block) and beside his Willoughby Road
 * paragraph in the design section (a wardrobe in sections, his own point that
 * the furniture can be made in sections for narrow access); the guide prices as
 * cards, each with its approved line on what moves the figure; the cost guide
 * sentence corrected (it gives alcove units by finish only). The design section
 * keeps its three AI renders; the building, listing and access sections take no
 * drawing. Plates: the three real photographs already on the page
 * (fitted-wardrobes-08, alcove-units-08, media-walls-02; the register's ssp-091,
 * ssp-103 and ssp-085), because the register's one Hampstead photograph,
 * ssp-004, is a portrait of the same wardrobe as the first (areas.ts).
 *
 * CHECKED 15 Nov by three independent reviewers (copy, facts, swap test), told
 * that his words are protected. Changed on their findings: the sections drawing
 * moved beside his sentence about sections; the first FAQ no longer repeats the
 * lede or Camden; the kitchens answer now answers; "where appropriate" and
 * "drawer systems" left the hardware answer (the cost guide's starting
 * specification has soft-close hinges); two drawing labels say only what is
 * drawn; the description no longer claims cutting to the walls.
 *
 * FAQ, all three new, from Google People also ask (UK, English, desktop, the
 * built-in browser, 15 Nov 2026): "What does bespoke joinery mean?" and "What
 * is high-end joinery?" (bespoke joinery hampstead, the area's second product
 * search, 32 impressions), "Are bespoke kitchens worth it?" (bespoke joinery).
 * The wardrobe searches' boxes held only costs and other companies. Answers
 * from what he approved: the materials page (the material range; the hardware
 * section) and the bespoke kitchens page (service-copy.ts, its intro and
 * Planning section). Dropped: "How long do the first visit and the survey
 * take?", "Can new furniture match the woodwork already in the house?", "How
 * long does a job take?" (not from a product search) and "What should I check
 * before hiring a carpenter?" (a carpenter question; the guarantee is above).
 */
import AreaPage from "../../components/AreaPage.astro";
import Wrap from "../../components/ds/Wrap.astro";
import SectionHead from "../../components/ds/SectionHead.astro";
import Reviews from "../../components/ds/Reviews.astro";
import Faq from "../../components/ds/Faq.astro";
import Link from "../../components/ds/Link.astro";
import Render from "../../components/journal/Render.astro";
import Renders from "../../components/journal/Renders.astro";
import AreaSide from "../../components/AreaSide.astro";
import PriceCards from "../../components/PriceCards.astro";

import { AREAS } from "../../data/areas";
import { QUOTE_BANK, REVIEWS } from "../../data/site-content";
import { ANSWERS, answered } from "../../data/answers";
import { photoText } from "../../data/plate-services";

const area = AREAS.find((a) => a.slug === "areas/hampstead")!;

/* The three renders in the design section: alt text and a visible description in the same words
   (a founder, 19 Nov 2026; src/data/plate-services.ts). Their approved alt text is the middle part.
   21 Nov 2026, Denis on the review link: the cornice pair (one wall, one view, only the top changed, lit
   shelves) broke the rule that compared pictures come from different rooms, and "remove the leds". The
   pair is now the alcove guide's two new renders, each a different room with no lighting (site PR #73,
   protocol section 8), with the guide's descriptions less its closing area sentence.
   26 Nov 2026: no description printed under the renders (K20; a founder, 24 Nov: "keep the photos but
   delete the description"), and each alt text cut to one sentence about the joinery (writing guide
   section 6). The longer descriptions were ours, not Kaspar's: the alcove pair's from 21 Nov, the sage
   wardrobe's rewritten "in his style" on 22 Oct (site e9836a4; his five renders were others). */
const R_BELOW_CORNICE = photoText("Alcove units", "Alcove units in warm mushroom grey: open shelving over flat-door cupboards with small black knobs, each stopping below the cornice so it stays visible.");
const R_CORNICE_ACROSS = photoText("Alcove units", "Alcove unit in muted blue-grey: open shelving over a two-door cupboard with aged-brass cup handles, and the cornice carried across its top.");
const R_HANDLELESS = photoText("Fitted wardrobes", "A wall-to-wall wardrobe with six full-height handleless flat doors in smooth sage, a slim infill up to the flat ceiling and a recessed plinth.", area.name);

/* The reviews: every bank entry tagged with this district, in bank order,
   whole. A gated entry takes its byline from its key once answered. */
const quotes = QUOTE_BANK.filter((q) => q.areas.includes(area.name)).map((q) => ({
  text: q.text,
  meta: q.gate && answered(q.gate) ? String(ANSWERS[q.gate].value) : q.meta,
}));

/* The approved cost list (first version), now as cards: each card's price and
   its line on what moves the figure are the list's own words. */
const PRICES = [
  {
    art: "fitted-wardrobes",
    name: "Fitted wardrobes",
    href: "/fitted-wardrobes/",
    price: "From £2,300 for a 2-door run up to 1.2m in melamine, and from £4,000 at 4 doors.",
    moves: "Width, finish, internal drawers, integrated lighting and more complex designs move the figure.",
  },
  {
    art: "alcove-units",
    name: "Alcove units",
    href: "/alcove-units/",
    price: "From £3,300 for a pair in melamine, and from £6,000 in oak veneer.",
    moves: "Finish, shelving detail, drawers and lighting move the figure. It assumes base cupboards with shelving over, up to about 1m per alcove.",
  },
  {
    art: "walk-in-wardrobes",
    name: "Walk-in wardrobes",
    href: "/walk-in-wardrobes/",
    price: "Typically from around £5,000.",
    moves: "The size of the room, number of fitted runs, internal layout, materials and finishes, and integrated lighting move the figure.",
  },
  {
    art: "tv-units",
    name: "Built-in TV units",
    href: "/media-walls/",
    price: "From £3,500.",
    moves: "Overall size, cabinetry and storage, integrated lighting, cable management and specialist finishes move the figure.",
  },
  {
    art: "kitchens",
    name: "Bespoke kitchens",
    href: "/bespoke-kitchens/",
    price: "Typically from £10,000.",
    moves: "Kitchen size and layout, cabinetry and internal storage, worktops, appliances, and materials and finishes move the figure.",
  },
];
const P2 = "margin-top:var(--space-10)";
const TEXT = "max-width:var(--measure)";
const SECTION = "padding-bottom:var(--section-y)";
---

<AreaPage area={area} illustrations>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    <p>
      {/* Not held since 9 Nov 2026: a founder ruled not to ask Kaspar about Hampstead jobs.
          This is his approved sentence, live on every service page. 26 Nov 2026: our "and other
          bespoke carpentry" (15 Nov) is out (ops D3), so it reads as he wrote it. */}
      We design and fit <Link href="/fitted-wardrobes/">wardrobes</Link> across north London, with much of our work in Hampstead, Belsize Park, Kentish Town, Camden and surrounding areas of north London.
    </p>
    <p style={P2}>
      {/* Kaspar's words, 10 Nov 2026 (his comment on the preview); the links are ours. One word changed:
          he wrote "a seamless finish", and "seamless" is a hard-banned word (tone protocol v1.4 section 5)
          that scripts/verify-build.mjs refuses in a production build. "Neat" is his own word for the same
          thing in his 8 Nov section below ("a neat, well-integrated finish"). 15 Nov 2026, the Primrose
          Hill model: his "Every project" is now "Every piece of bespoke joinery". 26 Nov 2026: back to
          his "Every project" (ops D3 retires the carpentry-and-joinery opening rule). */}
      We also design and install <Link href="/walk-in-wardrobes/">walk-in wardrobes</Link>, <Link href="/alcove-units/">alcove units</Link>, <Link href="/media-walls/">built-in TV units</Link> and <Link href="/bespoke-kitchens/">bespoke kitchens</Link>. Every project is made to measure, designed around the available space and carefully fitted to achieve a neat finish.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  {/* Kaspar's words, 8 Nov 2026 (his answer on the areaHampstead1 card). Denis, 21 Nov 2026: "Absolutely."
     at the start of the answer deleted (and no answer opens with a yes). */}
  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Period homes and mansion flats" level={4}>Can bespoke fitted furniture be installed in Hampstead period homes and mansion flats?</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing="mansion-block">
      <p>
        Bespoke fitted furniture is particularly well suited to period properties, where walls, floors and ceilings are not always perfectly level or square. Each installation is carefully measured and designed to make the most of the available space while complementing the character of the property.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        Hampstead has a wide variety of architectural styles, from Georgian houses on Church Row and Regency villas on Keats Grove to Victorian terraces and traditional mansion blocks. These properties often feature original architectural details, unusual alcoves and varying ceiling heights, making made-to-measure furniture a practical choice.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        We design and install bespoke wardrobes, alcove units, shelving and other fitted furniture in both period houses and mansion flats, taking care to achieve a neat, well-integrated finish.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  {/* Heading and first paragraph: Kaspar's (8 Nov, on the design-led copy). The
      body: Kaspar's rewrite of our draft (10 Nov), as he wrote it. The closing
      line to the guides is ours; he left it unchanged. */}
  <Wrap as="section" id="design" style={SECTION}>
    {/* "Design" is the only builder-chosen word on the page: every other section
        carries a short label above its heading, and this one needs one to match. */}
    <SectionHead meta="Design" level={4}>What fitted furniture works best in different Hampstead properties</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      {/* 19 Nov 2026, a founder: "remove all ai examples descriptions pls, from everywhere". Kaspar's
          note (10 Nov 2026) that opened this section, saying the pictures in it are AI-generated
          examples, is removed; the paragraph below now opens the section, so it loses its top margin. */}
      {/* 13 November 2026, a founder: one repeat cut. Kaspar's 10 Nov rewrite opened
          here with "Hampstead has a rich variety of architectural styles, from Georgian
          houses on Church Row and Regency villas on Keats Grove to Victorian terraces
          around Willoughby Road and traditional mansion blocks such as Northcote
          Mansions. These properties often have distinctive architectural features
          that need to be considered when designing bespoke fitted furniture." It
          repeated, almost word for word, his 8 Nov sentence in the Fit section above,
          which stays. Willoughby Road and Northcote Mansions are still named in his
          paragraphs below. Nothing else of his is changed; Kaspar is told, not asked. */}
      <p>
        In period properties, careful attention is given to how fitted furniture integrates with the room's existing features. Wardrobes and cabinetry can be designed to finish neatly below original cornices or incorporate matching decorative details where appropriate. The aim is to create practical storage that complements the character and proportions of the property.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        For listed buildings, any proposed alterations affecting their special architectural or historic interest may require listed building consent. We recommend confirming any necessary permissions with Camden Council before work begins.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Renders>
      <Render src="/images/journal/alcove-guide-below-cornice.webp" width={1536} height={1024} caption="Stopping below the cornice" short="Stopping below the cornice" pair alt={R_BELOW_CORNICE} />
      <Render src="/images/journal/alcove-guide-cornice-across.webp" width={1536} height={1024} caption="Cornice across the top" short="Cornice across the top" pair alt={R_CORNICE_ACROSS} />
    </Renders>
    <AreaSide drawing="wardrobe-sections">
      <p style={P2}>
        Many period properties around Willoughby Road have steps leading to the entrance, along with narrow hallways and staircases. When planning a fitted furniture installation, we consider access as well as the room itself. Our furniture can be manufactured in separate sections and assembled on-site, making it easier to transport through restricted spaces.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p style={P2}>
        In traditional mansion flats, such as those found at Northcote Mansions, bespoke fitted wardrobes can be designed to complement both classic and contemporary interiors. Whether the preference is for traditional shaker-style doors or modern handleless cabinetry, the design can be tailored to suit the room's proportions and the client's requirements.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <Render src="/images/journal/hampstead-sage-handleless-wardrobes.webp" width={1536} height={1024} caption="Handleless flat doors" short="Handleless flat doors" alt={R_HANDLELESS} />
    </div>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p style={P2}>
        For layouts, shelf spacing and materials, see our <Link href="/journal/alcove-units-guide/">alcove units guide</Link> and <Link href="/journal/built-in-wardrobe-ideas/">built-in wardrobe ideas</Link>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  {/* Kaspar's words, 8 Nov 2026 (his answer on the areaHampstead3 card). */}
  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="In your building" level={4}>How do installations work in period houses and managed apartment buildings?</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>
        Working in period properties and managed apartment buildings can require additional planning, particularly around access, deliveries, working hours and protecting the property.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        We normally work between 8am and 5pm on weekdays, although working hours can be adjusted to accommodate the client's preferences and any building requirements. Where necessary, we can coordinate with the building's managing agent and provide relevant insurance and safety documentation before installation begins.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        During installation, we take care to protect floors and surrounding areas, minimise dust and disruption, and keep the workspace clean and organised. When cutting materials indoors, we always use dust extraction equipment. We also make suitable arrangements for deliveries and access depending on the property.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  {/* Kaspar's words, 8 Nov 2026 (his answer on the areaHampstead2 card). */}
  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Listed homes" level={4}>Do conservation areas and listed buildings affect fitted furniture?</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>
        Many properties in Hampstead are located within conservation areas, including those around Church Row, Keats Grove and Downshire Hill. Being in a conservation area does not normally prevent homeowners from installing bespoke fitted furniture. Internal installations, such as fitted wardrobes, alcove units and shelving, generally do not require planning permission simply because a property is in a conservation area.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        Listed buildings have additional protections, including their interiors. If an installation affects the building's special architectural or historic character, listed building consent may be required. This can include alterations to original features such as historic panelling, fireplaces or fitted cupboards.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        If your property is listed, we recommend checking with Camden Council before work begins to establish whether any permissions are necessary. We take care to respect existing architectural features and design fitted furniture to complement the character of the property.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        For further information, visit <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/listed-buildings" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Camden Council's listed buildings guidance</Link>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  {/* Kaspar's words, 8 Nov 2026 (his answer on the areaHampstead4 card), then his
      rewrite of our parking line (10 Nov), as he wrote it; the two links are ours. */}
  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Access" level={4}>How do parking and access work for installations in Hampstead?</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>
        We discuss parking, deliveries and access arrangements with the client before installation begins. This includes identifying suitable loading and unloading areas and checking whether there are any access restrictions.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        Where necessary, we may ask the client to arrange parking permits or provide information about building access, lifts and any restrictions on deliveries. This helps us plan the installation efficiently and minimise disruption.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        Parking restrictions vary across Hampstead, with many streets falling within Camden's controlled parking zones, including CA-H. We check local parking arrangements before installation and discuss any permits or access requirements with the client where necessary. Further information about <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/controlled-parking-zones" target="_blank" rel="noopener">parking zones</Link> and <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/trade-permits" target="_blank" rel="noopener">trade permits</Link> is available on Camden Council's website.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Guide prices" level={4}>Guide prices for fitted furniture in Hampstead</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide>
      <p>Every figure here is a starting price, and a larger or more detailed project costs more.</p>
      <p style={P2}>
        A fixed, itemised quotation follows the measured survey. Our <Link href="/fitted-wardrobe-cost/">cost guide</Link> gives starting prices for fitted wardrobes by size and finish, and for alcove units by finish.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        {/* Arthur's comment, 7 Nov 2026 ("u can see the elevations to get an idea how the unit would look"), drafted
            9 Nov, then rewritten by Kaspar on 10 Nov: his words, as he wrote them; the process link is ours. */}
        For projects requiring detailed design work, we can provide scaled drawings and elevations to help you visualise the finished furniture before manufacturing begins. The level of design work depends on the complexity of the project and is discussed during the quotation process.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        <Link href="/process/">Our process</Link> typically includes an initial consultation and site survey, quotation and design, preparation and manufacturing, followed by installation.
      </p>
      <PriceCards slot="side" items={PRICES} />
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  {/* "From N Google reviews" and the link to all of them are the component's, from REVIEWS.count. */}
  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <Reviews
      title=""
      average={REVIEWS.average}
      count={REVIEWS.count}
      href={REVIEWS.profileUrl}
      quotes={quotes}
    />
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Guarantee" level={4}>What guarantee comes with our fitted furniture?</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>
        The guarantee covers installation and workmanship for 2 years and the structural integrity of the joinery for 10 years. Hinges, runners and other hardware carry their manufacturer's warranty. If doors, drawers or other moving parts require adjustment during the 2-year aftercare period, we will return and adjust them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Questions" scribe>Asked before enquiring</SectionHead>
    <div style="margin-top:var(--space-12);max-width:var(--measure);border-bottom:var(--rule-hair)">
      {/* 26 Nov 2026: the three answers rewritten to SS_WRITING_FOR_KASPAR.md (see the header note);
          the questions are unchanged. */}
      <Faq q="What does bespoke joinery mean?">
        Bespoke joinery is fitted furniture made to measure for one room, such as wardrobes, alcove units, shelving or a kitchen. It can combine materials, for example moisture-resistant MDF, natural wood veneer and melamine, to balance looks, durability and cost. We design it with you after the measured survey, then fit it in your home.
      </Faq>
      <Faq q="What is high-end joinery?">
        High-end joinery is fitted furniture where the materials and hardware are chosen to last as well as to look right. We specify hardware from Blum and Häfele, and soft-close hinges come as standard. That means doors close smoothly, drawers run properly and the hinges can be adjusted so the doors stay in line.
      </Faq>
      <Faq q="Are bespoke kitchens worth it?">
        A bespoke kitchen is worth it if you want the layout and storage planned around your room and what you keep in it. We design the cabinets, any tall cupboards or island, and the drawers and shelves inside them together, so they suit the room's proportions. Our bespoke kitchens typically start from £10,000.
      </Faq>
    </div>
  </Wrap>
</AreaPage>
